  "content": {
    "title": "When is First Four for March Madness? Schedule, dates, location for play-in games",
    "text": "March 15, 2026Updated March 16, 2026, 12:36 a.m. ETBring on March Madness.In just a few hours, the 68-team NCAA Tournament bracket will be revealed, marking the first step on the road to Indianapolis and the Final Four for teams. It will also allow the excitement, pageantry and tradition that comes annually with one of the best postseason tournaments in all of sports to officially get underway for fans.Included in that bracket reveal will be the lucky eight teams — depending on how you look at it — that get sent to the University of Dayton and UD Arena to partake in the two-night, play-in tournament that is the First Four.The First Four has delivered some fireworks over the years, while creating some moments of history for some programs, as the play-in tournament sometimes represents a program's first time competing on the March Madness stage — and possibly landing their first NCAA Tournament win.In 12 of the past 14 years that the First Four has been part of the March Madness format, at least one team that played its way into the 64-team field advanced to at least the second round. A fun example of this was Tobin Anderson's then-Fairleigh Dickinson Knights winning their First Four game and then upsetting No. 1 seed Purdue in like 48 hours.Who made the First Four in 2026? Here's what you need to know about the First Four for the men's NCAA Tournament, including a history of the First Four and more:Stream March Madness First Four games live with SlingTVWhen is the First Four for men's March Madness?Dates: Tuesday, March 17 through Wednesday, March 18The First Four of the Men's NCAA Tournament tips off Tuesday, March 17 at 6:40 p.m. ET. It will carry into Wednesday, March 18, and consist of four total games, with two games taking place on each day.For the two teams that win in Tuesday's First Four games, they will advance to first-round games Thursday, March 19 in the NCAA Tournament. For teams that win on Wednesday, they will advance to first-round games Friday, March 20. The winner of the No. 11 seed First Four game will play a No. 6 seed in the bracket, while the winner of the No. 16 seed games will play one of the No. 1 seeds.All told, those who win a First Four game have a rather quick turnaround to get to their respective first-round city for practice and game prep.Here's a breakdown of the First Four schedule for the 2026 NCAA Tournament:Tuesday, March 17Gamer 1: No. 16 UMBC vs. No. 16 Howard | 6:40 p.m. ETGame 2: No. 11 NC State vs. No. 11 Texas | 9:10 p.m. ETTV channel: truTVStreaming options: March Madness Live app and SlingTVWednesday, March 18Gamer 1: No. 16 Prairie View A&M vs. Lehigh | 6:40 p.m. ETGame 2: No. 11 Miami (OH) vs. SMU | 9:10 p.m. ETTV channel: truTVStreaming options: March Madness Live app and SlingTVStream March Madness First Four games live with SlingTVWhere is the First Four for men's March Madness held?Location: UD Arena (Dayton, Ohio)The First Four will take place once again on the campus of the University of Dayton at UD Arena in Dayton, Ohio.It is the 14th time that the First Four will take place inside the home area of the Flyers, as the play-in tournament was added to the NCAA Tournament format in 2011. Since its inception, the First Four has taken place at UD Arena in all but one NCAA Tournament: 2021, when the entire tournament was sent to Indiana due to COVID-19.Who plays in the First Four? Explaining March Madness play-in gamesThe First Four, historically, includes the four lowest-seeded automatic qualifiers (i.e., No. 16 seeds) and the four lowest-seeded at-large teams (i.e., those that were on the bubble in the \"last four in\" category). Perhaps the most common misconception about the First Four is how it fits into the NCAA Tournament. While it is the kickoff to March Madness, the games do not officially count for the first round of the tournament. They are instead they are play-in games to the tournament, which will be trimmed down to 64 teams by the time the first noon ET window game on Thursday, March 19 gets underway.For the four lowest-seeded at-large teams that get sent to Dayton, they are traditionally playing for an 11-seed in the larger NCAA Tournament. However, there have been some exceptions to that, as teams playing for the 10-, 12-, 13- and 14-seed lines have been sent to Dayton in the past.First Four history, game resultsVCU in 2011 and UCLA in 2021 are the two teams that have made it the farthest out of the First Four in the history of the NCAA Tournament.Here's a full list how past First Four games have gone since it began in 2011:2011(11) UTSA 70, Alabama State 61(12) Clemson 70, UAB 52(16) UNC-Asheville 81, Arkansas-Little Rock 77(16) VCU 59, USC 462012(12) South Florida 65, Cal 54(14) Vermont 71, Lamar 59(16) BYU 78, Iona 54(16) Western Kentucky 59, Mississippi Valley State2013(11) Saint Mary's 67, Middle Tennessee 54(13) La Salle 80, Boise State 71(16) North Carolina A&T 73, Liberty 72(16) James Madison 67, LIU-Brooklyn 552014(11) Tennessee 78, Iowa 65(12) NC State 74, Xavier 59(16) Albany 71, Mount St. Mary's 64(16) Cal Poly 81, Texas Southern 692015(11) Dayton 56, Boise State 55(11) Ole Miss 94, BYU 90(16) Hampton 74, Manhattan 64(16) Robert Morris 81, North Florida 772016(11) Michigan 67, Tulsa 62(11) Wichita State 70, Vanderbilt 50(16) Florida Gulf Coast 96, Fairleigh Dickinson 65(16) Holy Cross 59, Southern 552017(11) Kansas State 95, Wake Forest 88(11) USC 75, Providence 71(16) UC Davis 67, North Carolina Central 63(16) Mount St. Mary's 67, New Orleans 662018(11) St. Bonaventure 65, UCLA 58(11) Syracuse 60, Arizona State 56(16) Radford 71, LIU-Brooklyn 61(16) Texas Southern 64, North Carolina Central 462019(11) Arizona State 74, St. John's 65(11) Belmont 81, Temple 70(16) North Dakota State 78, North Carolina Central 74(16) Fairleigh Dickinson 82, Prairie View A&M 762021(11) UCLA 86, Michigan State 80(11) Drake 53, Wichita State 52(16) Norfolk State 54, Appalachian State 53(16) Texas Southern 60, Mount St. Mary's 522022(11) Notre Dame 89, Rutgers 87(12) Indiana 66, Wyoming 58(16) Texas Southern 76, Texas A&M Corpus Christi 67(16) Wright State 93, Bryant 822023(11) Pitt 60, Mississippi State 59(11) Arizona State 98, Nevada 73(16) Texas A&M Corpus Christi 75, SE Missouri State 71(16) Fairleigh Dickinson 84, Texas Southern 612024(10) Colorado State 67, (10) Virginia 42(10) Colorado 60, (10) Boise State 53(16) Wagner 71, (16) Howard 68(16) Grambling State 88, (16) Montana State 81 (OT)2025(11) Xavier 86, (11) Texas 80(11) North Carolina 95, (11) San Diego State 68(16) Alabama State 70, (16) Saint Francis 68(16) Mount St. Mary's 83, (16) American 72The USA TODAY app gets you to the heart of the news — fast. Download for award-winning coverage, crosswords, audio storytelling, the eNewspaper and more.",
